Z||000037||SLAMMED87GT||12-19-2001||02:08 PM||||Here's a pic of mine!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero10-01,5.jpg"> <P>I did not cut the decklid on mine however. It's just for looks now. I liked the "hemi-scoop" look so I painted it flat black.<P>I also got mine free from a local junkyard! It was the original design from an '80s mustang. I just unbolted it and wallaa! Same junkyard where I got my clear front parking lights from. I get pretty good deals at this place!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pfierosig2_1.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR>3" Drop, 17" Primax 618s/Nitto450 205/40, 4" Monza exhaust, Mudstain decklid scoop, 7" wing stands, Clear front and rear lights, custom interior, all options...that's all hehe.||||reg||6
Z||000038||PAUL88GT||12-21-2001||10:51 AM||||I would still like to know what the best tool for cutting the decklid.  I have a jigsaw, circular saw, and dremel tool.  I am hoping this job can be done with a jigsaw.  I am thinking about installing one on the 86SE I am restoring.<BR>Thanks and happy holidays.<BR>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000039||Devotshka||12-21-2001||01:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PAUL88GT:<BR><B>I would still like to know what the best tool for cutting the decklid.  I have a jigsaw, circular saw, and dremel tool. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Of the three, you are probably best off with the dremel.  It will be a slow process with that, however there are some places (at least on notchback, not sure on fastback) that need to be cut only through the top layer etc, not all the way through.  But like i said, with a GT i could be wrong on that one, since i don't think the hump interferes as much....  If you can get access to one, i'd use a air die-grinder with a cut off wheel.  Only takes about 5 minutes to get everything prepped that way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
